The key first move is the safe queen check Qc5-b5+, which cannot be captured by any black unit (the c4-pawn is irrelevant here; the b1-rook simply guards the queen). Black's only legal replies are king moves to c7, c8, d6, d8 or e7.  

Against the principal reply 1...Kc8 the immediate follow-up 2.Qb7# works:  
- the check is diagonal and adjacent, so it cannot be blocked;  
- Kc8 cannot capture on b7 because that square is protected by the b1-rook;  
- neither black rook nor the queen nor the bishop can capture on b7;  
- every flight square (b8, c7, d7) is controlled by the queen on b7 or the rook on b1.  

All other king flights are likewise met by an immediate mate (typically Rb7# or Qb7#), completing the forced mate in two.
